{
  "dom_id": "step-q48022",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at $x=27$, approximate the value of $\\sqrt[3]{28}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=27,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(27)+{f}^{′}(27)(x-27).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(27)$ and ${f}^{′}(27)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(27)=\\sqrt[3]{27}=3,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(27)=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{27}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{27}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\nL(x)=3+\\frac{1}{27}(x-27).\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt[3]{28}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=28.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{28} & ≈L(28) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{27}(28-27) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{27}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
